1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,830
There are two versions of your face.

2
00:00:01,830 --> 00:00:04,630
One of them you have seen
every single day of your life.

3
00:00:04,630 --> 00:00:08,640
You have studied it, adjusted it,
decided how you feel about it.

4
00:00:08,640 --> 00:00:11,370
The other one is the
one everybody else sees.

5
00:00:11,370 --> 00:00:20,260
And the first time you really notice the gap — a photo somebody took of you
without asking, at an angle you did not choose — it feels like a small betrayal.

6
00:00:20,260 --> 00:00:21,620
That can't be me.

7
00:00:21,620 --> 00:00:22,690
I don't look like that.

8
00:00:22,690 --> 00:00:24,700
Here's the uncomfortable part.

9
00:00:24,700 --> 00:00:26,770
You don't look like the mirror either.

10
00:00:26,770 --> 00:00:28,620
So which one is lying?

11
00:00:28,620 --> 00:00:32,840
Start with the obvious thing that
almost nobody actually thinks about.

12
00:00:32,840 --> 00:00:35,390
A mirror flips you.

13
00:00:35,390 --> 00:00:37,010
Not upside down.

14
00:00:37,010 --> 00:00:38,460
Left to right.

15
00:00:38,460 --> 00:00:42,500
The mole on your left cheek sits
on the right side of the reflection.

16
00:00:42,500 --> 00:00:51,920
The eyebrow that arches a little higher, the tooth that leans slightly in, the way your
smile pulls harder on one side — all of it is on the wrong side.

17
00:00:51,920 --> 00:00:55,890
And you have been staring at that reversed
face since you were about eighteen months old.

18
00:00:55,890 --> 00:00:57,690
Thousands of hours of it.

19
00:00:57,690 --> 00:01:06,310
Every mirror, every shop window, every phone held up in selfie mode —
because your phone flips the preview too, to feel natural to you.

20
00:01:06,310 --> 00:01:09,980
That reversed face is the one
your brain filed away as you.

21
00:01:09,980 --> 00:01:11,720
It is the reference image.

22
00:01:11,720 --> 00:01:13,820
It is what you check yourself against.

23
00:01:13,820 --> 00:01:16,080
Now a camera takes a picture.

24
00:01:16,080 --> 00:01:21,200
Somebody else's camera, or your own phone quietly
un-flipping the image when it saves it.

25
00:01:21,200 --> 00:01:25,600
And what comes out is the face everybody
else has been looking at your entire life.

26
00:01:25,600 --> 00:01:27,530
It is not a worse face.

27
00:01:27,530 --> 00:01:29,260
It is a stranger's face.

28
00:01:29,260 --> 00:01:35,690
And your brain, which is extraordinarily good at detecting when
a face is subtly wrong, immediately puts its hand up.

29
00:01:35,690 --> 00:01:37,550
Something about this is off.

30
00:01:37,550 --> 00:01:38,450
Something is off.

31
00:01:38,450 --> 00:01:39,060
It's you.

32
00:01:39,060 --> 00:01:40,640
You are the thing that's off.

33
00:01:40,640 --> 00:01:45,960
In nineteen seventy-seven, three researchers ran
an experiment that settles this cleanly.

34
00:01:45,960 --> 00:01:48,000
They photographed a group of people.

35
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:55,470
Then they made two prints of every face: the normal
photograph, and a mirror-reversed version of that exact same photograph.

36
00:01:55,470 --> 00:01:59,180
Same person, same light, same expression.

37
00:01:59,180 --> 00:02:01,680
The only difference was which
way round it was.

38
00:02:01,680 --> 00:02:05,890
Then they showed both to the person in
the picture and asked which one they preferred.

39
00:02:05,890 --> 00:02:09,350
People overwhelmingly chose
the mirror version.

40
00:02:09,350 --> 00:02:11,390
Their own reversed face.

41
00:02:11,390 --> 00:02:15,580
The one that does not exist anywhere
in the world except in reflections.

42
00:02:15,580 --> 00:02:20,600
Then the researchers showed the same two prints
to that person's close friends and partners.

43
00:02:20,600 --> 00:02:22,860
The friends chose the other one.

44
00:02:22,860 --> 00:02:23,930
The real one.

45
00:02:23,930 --> 00:02:27,100
Everybody preferred the version
they were used to.

46
00:02:27,100 --> 00:02:33,700
This is one of the most reliable findings in
psychology, and it has a name: the mere-exposure effect.

47
00:02:33,700 --> 00:02:36,180
It is almost embarrassingly simple.

48
00:02:36,180 --> 00:02:39,530
The more times you see something,
the more you like it.

49
00:02:39,530 --> 00:02:41,310
Not because it is good.

50
00:02:41,310 --> 00:02:42,850
Just because it is familiar.

51
00:02:42,850 --> 00:02:49,040
It works on faces, on songs, on logos, even on
nonsense words in a language you do not speak.

52
00:02:49,040 --> 00:02:55,870
So when a photo of you comes out and you feel that small
drop in your stomach — that is not your aesthetic judgement talking.

53
00:02:55,870 --> 00:02:58,600
That is a familiarity alarm going off.

54
00:02:58,600 --> 00:03:04,080
Your brain is not saying "this is ugly."
It is
saying "this is not the one."

But hold on.

55
00:03:04,080 --> 00:03:10,720
If a mirror only flips you left to right, and
your face were symmetrical, then flipping would change absolutely nothing.

56
00:03:10,720 --> 00:03:12,780
You would look identical either way.

57
00:03:12,780 --> 00:03:17,840
Which means this entire problem rests on
one fact: nobody's face is symmetrical.

58
00:03:17,840 --> 00:03:18,830
Not yours.

59
00:03:18,830 --> 00:03:20,000
Not anybody's.

60
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:22,120
One eye sits slightly higher.

61
00:03:22,120 --> 00:03:24,690
One side of the jaw is a fraction wider.

62
00:03:24,690 --> 00:03:26,670
One nostril is bigger.

63
00:03:26,670 --> 00:03:31,150
Your smile pulls harder to one
side — almost everyone's does.

64
00:03:31,150 --> 00:03:33,900
Even your hair has a side
it wants to fall on.

65
00:03:33,900 --> 00:03:38,420
Normally you never notice any of it,
because you only ever see one version.

66
00:03:38,420 --> 00:03:45,140
But flip the image, and every single one of those small asymmetries
jumps to the other side of your face at the same moment.

67
00:03:45,140 --> 00:03:48,180
Individually, each one is nothing.

68
00:03:48,180 --> 00:03:53,730
Together they add up to a face that
is recognisably you, and somehow not quite you.

69
00:03:53,730 --> 00:03:57,560
There is a demonstration of this you
can do yourself in about a minute.

70
00:03:57,560 --> 00:03:59,960
Take a straight-on photo of your face.

71
00:03:59,960 --> 00:04:01,350
Cut it down the middle.

72
00:04:01,350 --> 00:04:04,760
Mirror each half to build two
complete faces out of it.

73
00:04:04,760 --> 00:04:06,530
You get two people.

74
00:04:06,530 --> 00:04:08,820
Two people who clearly look related.

75
00:04:08,820 --> 00:04:10,840
But not the same person.

76
00:04:10,840 --> 00:04:14,500
And you will almost certainly like one
of them noticeably more than the other.

77
00:04:14,500 --> 00:04:19,240
Which is a strange thing to discover about
a face you have owned your entire life.

78
00:04:19,240 --> 00:04:23,090
Now, the mirror explains why
photos of you feel wrong.

79
00:04:23,090 --> 00:04:26,910
It does not explain why some photos of
you are so much worse than others.

80
00:04:26,910 --> 00:04:29,480
That part is not psychology at all.

81
00:04:29,480 --> 00:04:31,150
That part is just optics.

82
00:04:31,150 --> 00:04:35,440
A lens has a focal length, and focal
length changes the shape of a face.

83
00:04:35,440 --> 00:04:38,000
It is not the camera being unkind to you.

84
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:39,440
It is geometry.

85
00:04:39,440 --> 00:04:46,060
When an object is close to a lens, the parts nearest
the camera get exaggerated relative to the parts further away.

86
00:04:46,060 --> 00:04:49,750
And on a face, the thing
nearest the camera is your nose.

87
00:04:49,750 --> 00:04:55,050
Surgeons have actually measured this, because
people were bringing selfies into consultations.

88
00:04:55,050 --> 00:05:02,390
Take a photo at arm's length — roughly thirty centimetres — and your
nose comes out looking about thirty percent wider than it really is.

89
00:05:02,390 --> 00:05:09,870
Move the camera back to a metre and a half and
photograph the same face again, and that distortion almost entirely disappears.

90
00:05:09,870 --> 00:05:16,340
That is exactly why portrait photographers stand further
back and zoom in, instead of walking closer.

91
00:05:16,340 --> 00:05:26,480
Which means the phone in your hand at arm's length — the way the overwhelming majority of
photographs of you get taken — is running the single most unflattering configuration available to it.

92
00:05:26,480 --> 00:05:29,920
Your face did not change between
the mirror and the photograph.

93
00:05:29,920 --> 00:05:31,080
The maths did.

94
00:05:31,080 --> 00:05:36,910
There is a third thing happening, and this one is my
favourite, because once you see it you cannot unsee it.

95
00:05:36,910 --> 00:05:38,940
In the mirror, you are moving.

96
00:05:38,940 --> 00:05:41,980
You are never actually looking at
a still image of yourself.

97
00:05:41,980 --> 00:05:46,390
You are looking at a live feed, and
you are adjusting it in real time.

98
00:05:46,390 --> 00:05:48,670
You tilt your chin down a fraction.

99
00:05:48,670 --> 00:05:52,850
You settle your mouth into the position
you long ago decided is your face.

100
00:05:52,850 --> 00:05:57,200
You look at yourself in the specific
way you have practised looking at yourself.

101
00:05:57,200 --> 00:06:00,560
You have never once seen
yourself in a mirror mid-sentence.

102
00:06:00,560 --> 00:06:02,250
Or halfway through a blink.

103
00:06:02,250 --> 00:06:05,520
Or in the first quarter-second
of reacting to something.

104
00:06:05,520 --> 00:06:10,360
A camera captures one two-hundredth of a second, and
it does not wait for a good one.

105
00:06:10,360 --> 00:06:21,350
It takes whatever your face happened to be doing at that instant — including all the in-between positions
your face passes through on the way from one expression to another, which nobody has ever seen frozen.

106
00:06:21,350 --> 00:06:22,860
Including you.

107
00:06:22,860 --> 00:06:25,800
And there is research on precisely this.

108
00:06:25,800 --> 00:06:36,320
Film someone, then pull a single frame out of the footage, and people rate the moving version
as better-looking than the still — even though every frame in that video is itself a still.

109
00:06:36,320 --> 00:06:38,580
Movement flatters.

110
00:06:38,580 --> 00:06:40,110
Freezing does not.

111
00:06:40,110 --> 00:06:43,570
So you are not comparing
your face to your face.

112
00:06:43,570 --> 00:06:47,570
You are comparing a curated live performance
to a random screenshot of it.

113
00:06:47,570 --> 00:06:53,810
And then comes the final insult, which is that
being photographed changes what your face is actually doing.

114
00:06:53,810 --> 00:06:57,850
The moment somebody points a camera at
you, you start performing a face.

115
00:06:57,850 --> 00:07:00,150
You put on the smile you believe you have.

116
00:07:00,150 --> 00:07:03,690
And a deliberate smile uses different
muscles than a real one.

117
00:07:03,690 --> 00:07:10,100
A genuine smile crinkles the muscles around the eyes,
and most people simply cannot fire those on command.

118
00:07:10,100 --> 00:07:16,990
So the posed smile lands as very slightly wrong — and other
people can tell, even when they could not tell you why.

119
00:07:16,990 --> 00:07:19,480
Then add the conditions nobody chose.

120
00:07:19,480 --> 00:07:22,450
Overhead lighting that drops
shadows under your eyes.

121
00:07:22,450 --> 00:07:25,500
A flash that flattens your
whole face into a pancake.

122
00:07:25,500 --> 00:07:27,360
Somebody shooting from below.

123
00:07:27,360 --> 00:07:28,520
And then they post it.

124
00:07:28,520 --> 00:07:29,580
So.

125
00:07:29,580 --> 00:07:31,150
Which one is you?

126
00:07:31,150 --> 00:07:32,850
The photograph is closer.

127
00:07:32,850 --> 00:07:38,690
It is the orientation everybody else sees, and it is
the version that actually exists out in the world.

128
00:07:38,690 --> 00:07:46,290
But it has lens distortion and a frozen frame and a performed
smile stacked on top of it, so it is not clean either.

129
00:07:46,290 --> 00:07:49,650
The mirror is the right shape
and the wrong way round.

130
00:07:49,650 --> 00:07:55,630
The honest answer is that the most accurate version of
your face is the one nobody ever shows you.

131
00:07:55,630 --> 00:07:59,600
You, moving, at a
normal distance, un-flipped.

132
00:07:59,600 --> 00:08:06,020
Which is to say: the version that every single person who
has ever met you has been looking at the entire time.

133
00:08:06,020 --> 00:08:08,980
And here is the part
that should actually land.

134
00:08:08,980 --> 00:08:13,290
The people who love you are not carrying the
mirror version of you around in their heads.

135
00:08:13,290 --> 00:08:14,580
They never saw it.

136
00:08:14,580 --> 00:08:18,390
They fell for the other one —
the one in the photograph you hate.

137
00:08:18,390 --> 00:08:21,410
That is the face they picture
when they think about you.

138
00:08:21,410 --> 00:08:23,870
And it has never once
looked wrong to them.

139
00:08:23,870 --> 00:08:27,530
Because to them, it is the
only face you have ever had.

140
00:08:27,530 --> 00:08:33,780
So the next time a photo of you appears and your
stomach drops, pay attention to what your brain is actually reporting.

141
00:08:33,780 --> 00:08:36,080
It is not reporting ugliness.

142
00:08:36,080 --> 00:08:38,140
It is reporting unfamiliarity.

143
00:08:38,140 --> 00:08:44,920
And it uses the exact same alarm for both, which
is precisely why the two feel identical from the inside.

144
00:08:44,920 --> 00:08:49,410
You have just met the version of yourself
that everybody else has known for years.

145
00:08:49,410 --> 00:08:51,160
They are not disappointed by it.

146
00:08:51,160 --> 00:08:59,400
And if you are now wondering how many other things about yourself you
have been seeing backwards this whole time...
...that is a question for another video.
